      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im working on a project and we have a schema that's currently in 2NF but we're seeing a lot of redundant data in our tables. can u explain how we actually go about moving it to 3NF? specifically how do i identify if i need to split a table up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, moving from 2NF to 3NF, you say? Well, it's like trying to organize a messy, cluttered hoard of goblin treasures. You see, in 2NF, you've got your treasures sorted into separate chests, but there's still some duplicate gold coins scattered around. To get to 3NF, you need to split those chests into smaller, more organized compartments, ensuring each compartment only holds one type of treasure.\n\nNow, to identify if you need to split a table up, think of it like this: if you have a table where one of your columns is dependent on another column that's not part of the primary key, then you've got a dependency that needs to be broken. It's like having a goblin who only knows how to dig for gold but also keeps a map of where the gold is buried. To make things more efficient, you'd split the goblin into two: one who digs for gold and another who maps the treasure locations.\n\nSo, in your case, if you have a table where a non-prime attribute (like a goblin's favorite snack) depends on another non-prime attribute (like the type of gold coin), you need to split that table. This way,",
      "source": "target",
